Abstract
Parkinson’s disease is a progressive neurodegenerative disease. It has severe effects on motor and speech functions and has an acute need to find precise and interpretable diagnostic tools. In this research, the paper presents a neural network algorithmic representation of Parkinson’s disease detection on the basis of speech biomarkers. The strategy uses the DeepNetX2 model under two setups: a baseline model and an improved methodology, which involves Spearman correlation-based feature selection, normalization, dropout regularization, L2 penalties, and RMSprop optimization. Experimental analyses of the UCI Parkinson speech dataset indicate that the trained DeepNetX2 attains a better performance with 94.74% of accuracy and 96.07% area under the curve, compared with the vanilla implementation and the previous machine learning benchmarks. Explainable artificial intelligence methods made sure that there was transparency and used such tools as SHAP and LIME interpretability, which emphasized important speech features contributing to classification. These findings highlight the promise of streamlined deep learning pipelines with clinical decision support and encourage future studies on multimodal approaches that integrate speech and EEG data.
1. Introduction
Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a neurodegenerative disease characterized by progressive growth, which affects millions of people across the world, with symptoms including motor dysfunction and cognitive impairment. The increase in the prevalence of PD, which is accompanied by the lack of a clear cure, has put a high value on prompt and clear diagnosis. Traditional diagnostic methods tend to be subjective and require a lot of clinical experience; thus, the process is likely to be inconsistent and late in early detection. Computational intelligence approaches have therefore been proposed to be useful alternatives in the objective PD diagnosis. Initial applications of machine learning (ML) models showed how computational methods could be used to differentiate PD patients and healthy controls through measurable biomarkers such as speech, handwriting, and motion signs.1 The voice and speech signals are among these biomarkers that have been of specific interest because they are non-invasive and cost-effective. Speech impairment and vocal degradation are among the early observable symptoms of Parkinson’s disease and can provide important cues for early detection. Recent work has used ML algorithms to extract and analyze the voice features that are strongly correlated with vocal degradation in PD patients.2 Although these methods have shown promise, most have been constrained by a relative dearth of accuracy, non-generalizability, and the use of shallow ML classifiers, which are incapable of reflecting more complex features. Meanwhile, with the growing complexity of predictive models, the need to consider interpretability in healthcare applications has become a clear issue. Even though high predictive performance can be attained powered by the sophisticated ML and deep learning, the black-box nature of the predictive model raises issues of transparency and trust. To help solve these problems, explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) is proposed as a key paradigm that offers a balance between predictive accuracy and clinical interpretability. As an example, SHAP and LIME methods have been discussed in the medical field to elucidate model forecasts and emphasize the importance of features, which proves that XAI can be effective in improving the confidence of professionals.3 XAI-based structures have started delivering encouraging results in the scenario of PD detection. Recent studies that have used explainable voice-based analysis have shown that adding interpretability to the ML models can lead to not only a correct classification but also information on the most pertinent acoustic features linked to PD.4 Nevertheless, some of the current research still has limitations in the form of small data sets, unreliable cross-fold performance, and a lack of exploration of sophisticated deep learning models. Such constraints encourage the further enhancement of strong frameworks capable of enhancing accuracy without losing interpretability for clinical application.
2. Related work
The increased use of computational intelligence in neurodegenerative disease studies has given rise to various methodologies for diagnosing Parkinson’s disease (PD). As biomedical data are made available, investigators have resorted to the application of novel ML and deep learning algorithms in early diagnosis, both in classical modalities and new biomarkers. The recent literature has emphasized the expanded application of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) in the diagnosis of neurodegenerative disorders. To illustrate this point, Wang et al.5 presented an overview of the XAI applications in neurodegenerative diseases in a holistic manner, noting that XAI is important in terms of transparency and clinical trust. Such a view stresses the importance of the explainability of PD research in which correct predictions might not be enough without interpretable outcomes for clinicians. Imaging-based techniques have demonstrated potential, as they are specific to PD. Pant et al.6 suggested a model that fuses deep learning-based feature extraction and ML classifiers with DaTscan images that showed a competitive predictive accuracy. Equally, Magesh et al.7 tested an explainable ML model with the LIME on the DaTscan image and confirmed the capacity of the interpretable frameworks to support the early detection of PD. Although these imaging-based approaches have shown excellent performance, they are restricted in scalability and are only accessible to routine clinical practice due to the use of special medical scans. There are also multimodal studies that focus on enhancing the accuracy of classification. A contrastive graph cross-view learning strategy to combine SPECT images with clinical features was first presented by Ding et al.,8 where it was demonstrated that multimodal fusion improves diagnostic reliability. In addition to imaging, reviews like the one by Aziz et al.9 have also indicated the growing role of XAI in healthcare and PD in particular, where accuracy and interpretability act as focal points of concern. Voice-based detection feature optimization methods are on the increase in the non-invasive and more approachable type of biomarkers. Aarti et al.10 also designed a deep learning model along with a dynamic bag-of-features optimization method and found that this method enhanced feature selection and classification among PD patients. These writings show that smart refinement of features can be useful in improving the performance of such models, especially when the data are high-dimensional. In general, the previous studies highlight two important trends: the development of shallow ML to deep learning and the trend of adding the concept of explainability to the decision-support framework. Even with these developments, there is still a disparity in integrating deep representation learning, feature optimization, and interpretability within the same framework, which detects speech-based PD, which inspired the methodology used in the proposed research. To conduct the literature survey, a systematic search of various academic databases was conducted, which included PubMed, Scopus, IEEE Xplore, and Google Scholar. The search was based on the 2018
−
2025 publishing year to cover both the old and the new studies. Relevant articles were retrieved with respect to such key words as Parkinson’s disease detection, speech biomarkers, deep learning models, explainable AI, and biomedical feature selection. Such a thorough methodology allowed identifying previous attempts in voice-based biomarkers, feature engineering approaches, and explanatory frameworks, which form the basis of the methodological advances that are provided in this paper. Figure 1 depicts the schematic structure. Although imaging-based studies have demonstrated strong diagnostic performance, speech-based biomarkers offer a non-invasive and cost-effective alternative that enables scalable screening. Recent studies have shown that acoustic features such as jitter, shimmer, Mel Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs), and harmonic-to-noise ratio HNR provide meaningful indicators of Parkinson’s disease progression. Therefore, the present study focuses specifically on speech-based biomarkers while incorporating explainable AI methods to improve interpretability.
