4.3. Cross-validation and robustness analysis
The efficiency of the improved DeepNetX2 structure was checked with the help of a 10-fold stratified cross-validation procedure. The model showed a stable mean accuracy of 93.68% 
±
 1.45 and an AUC of 95.21% (
±
1.12) at different partitions of the data, thus indicating a strong consistency across different data partitions. This reliability means that the improvements are unlikely to be attributable to random variation or a particular train-test split according to a particular train, but we can use the improvement across unknown patient distributions. To examine the contribution of each methodological component, ablation studies were also run as well to study them.26–28 This held because the removal of the technique of selecting features based on their Spearman correlation lessened the accuracy by almost 3 percent, proving that dimensionality reduction was critical in the elimination of noise in the acoustic space. On the same note, the removal of the terms dropout and L2 regularization led to huge jumps in overfitting, which substantiates their necessity in the compromise of complexity and generalization of the models. Lastly, when substituting the use of RMSprop with the Adam optimizer, the convergence was unstable as well, which supports the feasibility of RMSprop in the present biomedical classification problem. Table 2 shows that the vanilla DeepNetX2 did not generalize and had overfitting, and the enhanced pipeline had the highest performance level compared to the other baselines and ablation variants, with the highest accuracy, sensitivity, and interpretability rates. Introduction of the so-called explainable AI (XAI) features of the SHAP and LIME not only made the model more trustworthy but also created a difference between it and purely predictive architectures. Taken together, the results in this article indicate that the improved DeepNetX2 is a clinically relevant and technically sound solution to the problem of detecting Parkinson’s disease - linking the high-quality acoustic biomarkers directly to understandable medical AI systems.
